{"step_index":301,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-09T12:31:51Z","content":"I have checked **`172.30.231.17`** on port `30` from your Debian Trixie host. Here is the diagnostic report:\n\n---\n\n### Diagnostic Findings for `172.30.231.17`\n\n1. **Network Connectivity**:\n   - Port `30` is <span style=\"color:green\">**open and reachable**</span> from your workstation (`Connection to 172.30.231.17 30 succeeded`).\n   - The ED25519 host key was verified and recorded in your `~/.ssh/known_hosts`.\n2. **Remote SSH Daemon**:\n   - **Banner**: Outremer Telecom PROD infrastructure banner confirmed.\n   - **Daemon**: `OpenSSH_10.0p2 Debian-7+deb13u4` (Debian 13 Trixie based).\n   - **Supported Authentication**: Both `publickey` and `password` are accepted.\n3. **Account State**:\n   - `koby-bowser-ansible`: Not yet present or configured on this server.\n   - `superuser`: Accessible on port 30, requiring password authentication (as expected).\n\n---\n\n### Script Update for Password-Based Bootstrap (`-K` / `--ask-pass`)\n\nBecause automated AI subshells do not hold or store interactive credentials, I enhanced [copy-to-remote.sh](file:///home/koby/.ansible/scripts/copy-to-remote.sh) with a secure **`-K` / `--ask-pass`** flag:\n\n- Prompts you for the `superuser` password masked in your terminal (`read -s`).\n- Uses `sshpass` and pipes securely to `sudo -S` without writing the password to any disk, log, or shell history.\n- Bootstraps `koby-bowser-ansible`, assigns groups (`SSH_Users` / `sudo`), installs your Debian Trixie Ed25519 public key ([~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub](file:///home/koby/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub)), and immediately `unsets` the password from memory.\n- Executes the Ansible playbook to deliver the file to `/tmp`.\n\n---\n\n### How to Run\n\nFrom your terminal in `~/.ansible`, run:\n\n```bash\n./scripts/copy-to-remote.sh -p 30 -a superuser -K 172.30.231.17 /path/to/your/file\n```\n\n> **What to Expect**:\n> 1. It will prompt